WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.399
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.399 --> 00:00:09.870
In this series, we talk a lot about different
AI algorithms that solve a variety of super

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.379
difficult tasks.

00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:16.480
These are typically tested within a software
environment in the form of a simulation program.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.270
However, this often leaves the question open
whether these algorithms would really work

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.970
in real world environments.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:24.360
So what about that?

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.480
This work from OpenAI goes by the name domain
randomization, and is about training an AI

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.480
on relatively crude computer simulations in
a way that can be transferred to the real

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.480
world.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.960
The problem used to demonstrate this was localizing
and grasping objects.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.650
Note that this algorithm has never seen any
real images and was trained using simulated

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:47.650
data.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.920
It only played a computer game, if you will.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.970
Now, the question we immediately think about
is what the term domain randomization has

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.989
to do with transferring simulation knowledge
into reality?

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:04.110
The key observation is that using simulated
training data is okay, but we have to make

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:10.100
sure that the AI is exposed to a diverse enough
set of circumstances to obtain knowledge that

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.159
generalizes properly, hence the term domain
randomization.

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:17.950
In these experiments, the following parameters
were heavily randomized:

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:23.289
number of shapes and distractor objects on
the table, positions and textures on the objects,

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:28.659
table and the environment, number of lights,
material properties, and the algorithm was

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:31.850
even exposed to some random noise as well
in the images.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.340
And it turns out that if we do this properly,
leaning on the knowledge of only a few thousand

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:42.799
images, when the algorithm is uploaded to
a real robot arm, it becomes capable of grasping

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:44.890
the correct prescribed objects.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.079
In this case, the objective was, spam detection.

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:49.079
Very amusing.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:54.520
I think the very interesting part is that
it is not even using photorealistic rendering

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.859
and light simulations - these programs are
able to create high quality images that resemble

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:04.659
the real world around us, and it is mostly
clear that those would be useful to train

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:05.959
such an algorithm.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:12.010
However, this only uses extremely crude data
and the knowledge of the AI still generalizes

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.090
to the real world.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.629
How about that!

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

